1
00:00:01,799 --> 00:00:06,119
Chapter 10. a bosom friend.

2
00:00:06,120 --> 00:00:11,158
Returning to the Spouter Inn from the chapel, I found Quiqu there quite alone,

3
00:00:11,159 --> 00:00:15,718
he having left the chapel before the benediction some time. He was sitting on

4
00:00:15,719 --> 00:00:19,958
a bench before the fire with his feet on the stove hearth, and in one hand was

5
00:00:19,959 --> 00:00:24,759
holding close to his face that little negro idol of his, peering hard into its

6
00:00:24,760 --> 00:00:29,238
face, and with a jack knife gently whittling away at its nose, meanwhile

7
00:00:29,239 --> 00:00:34,519
humming to himself in his heathenish way. But being now interrupted, he put

8
00:00:34,520 --> 00:00:39,317
up the image, and pretty soon, going to the table, took up a large book there,

9
00:00:39,318 --> 00:00:43,238
and placing it on his lap, began counting the pages with deliberate

10
00:00:43,239 --> 00:00:48,839
regularity, at every 50th page, as I fancied, stopping a moment, looking

11
00:00:48,840 --> 00:00:53,238
vacantly around him, and giving utterance to a longdrawn, gurgling

12
00:00:53,239 --> 00:00:58,199
whistle of astonishment. He would then begin again at the next 50, seeming to

13
00:00:58,200 --> 00:01:03,078
commence at the number one each time, as though he could not count more than 50,

14
00:01:03,079 --> 00:01:07,798
and it was only by such a large number of 50s being found together, that his

15
00:01:07,799 --> 00:01:13,317
astonishment at the multitude of pages was excited. With much interest I sat

16
00:01:13,318 --> 00:01:18,519
watching him, savage though he was, and hideously marred about the face, at

17
00:01:18,520 --> 00:01:22,759
least to my taste, his countenance yet had something in it which was by no

18
00:01:22,760 --> 00:01:27,718
means disagreeable. You cannot hide the soul. Through all his unearly

19
00:01:27,719 --> 00:01:32,519
tattooings, I thought I saw the traces of a simple, honest heart, and in his

20
00:01:32,520 --> 00:01:37,317
large, deep eyes, fiery black and bold, there seemed tokens of a spirit that

21
00:01:37,318 --> 00:01:41,878
would dare a thousand devils. And besides all this, there was a certain

22
00:01:41,879 --> 00:01:46,279
lofty bearing about the pagan, which even his uncoutheness could not

23
00:01:46,280 --> 00:01:50,839
altogether maim. He looked like a man who had never cringed, and had never had

24
00:01:50,840 --> 00:01:56,279
a creditor. Whether it was too that his head being shaved, his forehead was

25
00:01:56,280 --> 00:02:00,999
drawn out in freer and brighter relief, and looked more expansive than it

26
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:06,039
otherwise would. This I will not venture to decide, but certain it was. His head

27
00:02:06,040 --> 00:02:10,999
was frenologically an excellent one. It may seem ridiculous, but it reminded me

28
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:16,919
of General Washington's head, as seen in the popular busts of him. It had the

29
00:02:16,920 --> 00:02:21,638
same long, regularly graded, retreating slope from above the brows, which were

30
00:02:21,639 --> 00:02:26,839
likewise very projecting like two long promonitories thickly wooded on top.

31
00:02:26,840 --> 00:02:31,798
Quiquake was George Washington, cannibalistically developed.

32
00:02:31,799 --> 00:02:35,798
Whilst I was thus closely scanning him, half pretending, meanwhile, to be

33
00:02:35,799 --> 00:02:40,199
looking out at the storm from the casement, he never heeded my presence,

34
00:02:40,200 --> 00:02:44,358
never troubled himself with so much as a single glance, but appeared wholly

35
00:02:44,359 --> 00:02:48,919
occupied with counting the pages of the marvelous book. Considering how sociably

36
00:02:48,920 --> 00:02:53,078
we had been sleeping together the night previous, and especially considering the

37
00:02:53,079 --> 00:02:57,558
affectionate arm I had found thrown over me waking in the morning, I thought this

38
00:02:57,559 --> 00:03:03,078
indifference of his very strange. But savages are strange beings. At times you

39
00:03:03,079 --> 00:03:07,958
do not know exactly how to take them. At first they are overawing. Their calm

40
00:03:07,959 --> 00:03:13,558
self-clectiveness of simplicity seems a socratic wisdom. I had noticed also that

41
00:03:13,559 --> 00:03:18,119
Quiquake never consorted at all or but very little with the other seaman in the

42
00:03:18,120 --> 00:03:23,158
inn. He made no advances whatever appeared to have no desire to enlarge

43
00:03:23,159 --> 00:03:28,119
the circle of his acquaintances. All this struck me as mighty singular. Yet

44
00:03:28,120 --> 00:03:31,958
upon second thoughts, there was something almost sublime in it. Here was

45
00:03:31,959 --> 00:03:34,919
a man some 20,000 mi from home by way of

46
00:03:34,920 --> 00:03:39,558
Cape Horn, that is, which was the only way he could get there, thrown among

47
00:03:39,559 --> 00:03:43,798
people as strange to him as though he were in the planet Jupiter. And yet he

48
00:03:43,799 --> 00:03:48,599
seemed entirely at his ease, preserving the utmost serenity, content with his

49
00:03:48,600 --> 00:03:54,119
own companionship, always equal to himself. Surely this was a touch of fine

50
00:03:54,120 --> 00:03:58,919
philosophy, though no doubt he had never heard there was such a thing as that.

51
00:03:58,920 --> 00:04:03,638
But perhaps to be true philosophers, we mortals should not be conscious of so

52
00:04:03,639 --> 00:04:08,919
living or so striving. So soon as I hear that such and such a man gives himself

53
00:04:08,920 --> 00:04:14,679
out for a philosopher, I conclude that like the dispic old woman, he must have

54
00:04:14,680 --> 00:04:17,477
broken his digesttor.

55
00:04:17,478 --> 00:04:22,999
As I sat there in that now lonely room, the fire burning low in that mild stage,

56
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:27,477
when after its first intensity has warmed the air, it then only glows to be

57
00:04:27,478 --> 00:04:32,119
looked at, the evening shades and phantoms gathering round the casements,

58
00:04:32,120 --> 00:04:37,477
and peering in upon us silent solitary twain, the storm booming without in

59
00:04:37,478 --> 00:04:43,477
solemn swells. I began to be sensible of strange feelings. I felt a melting in

60
00:04:43,478 --> 00:04:47,798
me. No more my splintered heart and maddened hand were turned against the

61
00:04:47,799 --> 00:04:53,798
wolfish world. This soothing savage had redeemed it. There he sat, his very

62
00:04:53,799 --> 00:04:58,358
indifference speaking a nature in which there lurked no civilized hypocrisies

63
00:04:58,359 --> 00:05:04,679
and bland deceits. Wild he was a very sight of sights to see. Yet I began to

64
00:05:04,680 --> 00:05:09,558
feel myself mysteriously drawn towards him. And those same things that would

65
00:05:09,559 --> 00:05:14,919
have repelled most others, they were the very magnets that thus drew me. I'll try

66
00:05:14,920 --> 00:05:19,477
a pagan friend, thought I, since Christian kindness has proved but hollow

67
00:05:19,478 --> 00:05:24,599
courtesy. I drew my bench near him, and made some friendly signs and hints,

68
00:05:24,600 --> 00:05:26,279
doing my best to talk with him.

69
00:05:26,280 --> 00:05:31,397
Meanwhile, at first he little noticed these advances, but presently upon my

70
00:05:31,398 --> 00:05:35,477
referring to his last night's hospitalities, he made out to ask me

71
00:05:35,478 --> 00:05:38,039
whether we were again to be bedfellows.

72
00:05:38,040 --> 00:05:42,199
I told him yes, where I thought he looked pleased, perhaps a little

73
00:05:42,200 --> 00:05:46,438
complimented. We then turned over the book together, and I endeavored to

74
00:05:46,439 --> 00:05:50,119
explain to him the purpose of the printing and the meaning of the few

75
00:05:50,120 --> 00:05:54,999
pictures that were in it. Thus I soon engaged his interest, and from that we

76
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:59,238
went to jabbering the best we could about the various outer sightes to be

77
00:05:59,239 --> 00:06:04,199
seen in this famous town. Soon I proposed a social smoke, and producing

78
00:06:04,200 --> 00:06:08,919
his pouch and tomahawk, he quietly offered me a puff, and then we sat

79
00:06:08,920 --> 00:06:15,558
exchanging puffs from that wild pipe of his, and keeping it regularly passing between us.

80
00:06:15,559 --> 00:06:19,558
If there yet lurked any ice of indifference toward me in the pagan's

81
00:06:19,559 --> 00:06:24,438
breast, this pleasant, genial smoke we had soon thawed it out, and left us

82
00:06:24,439 --> 00:06:30,039
cronies. He seemed to take to me quite as naturally and unbiddenly as I to him,

83
00:06:30,040 --> 00:06:34,599
and when our smoke was over, he pressed his forehead against mine, clasped me

84
00:06:34,600 --> 00:06:39,238
around the waist, and said that henceforth we were married, meaning in

85
00:06:39,239 --> 00:06:43,958
his country's phrase that we were bosom friends. He would gladly die for me, if

86
00:06:43,959 --> 00:06:48,119
the need should be. In a countryman, this sudden flame of friendship would

87
00:06:48,120 --> 00:06:52,839
have seemed far too premature, a thing to be much distrusted. But in this

88
00:06:52,840 --> 00:06:58,199
simple savage, those old rules would not apply. After supper, and another social

89
00:06:58,200 --> 00:07:02,679
chat and smoke, we went to our room together. He made me a present of his

90
00:07:02,680 --> 00:07:07,558
imbalmed head, took out his enormous tobacco wallet, and groping under the

91
00:07:07,559 --> 00:07:10,839
tobacco, drew out some $30 in silver.

92
00:07:10,840 --> 00:07:14,279
then spreading them on the table and mechanically dividing them into two

93
00:07:14,280 --> 00:07:19,238
equal portions, pushed one of them toward me and said it was mine. I was

94
00:07:19,239 --> 00:07:23,078
going to remmonstrate, but he silenced me by pouring them into my trousers

95
00:07:23,079 --> 00:07:28,839
pockets. I let them stay. He then went about his evening prayers, took out his

96
00:07:28,840 --> 00:07:31,477
idol, and removed the paper fireboard.

97
00:07:31,478 --> 00:07:35,958
By certain signs and symptoms, I thought he seemed anxious for me to join him.

98
00:07:35,959 --> 00:07:39,958
But well, knowing what was to follow, I deliberated a moment whether in case he

99
00:07:39,959 --> 00:07:43,638
invited me, I would comply, or otherwise.

100
00:07:43,639 --> 00:07:48,599
I was a good Christian, born and bred in the bosom of the infallible Presbyterian

101
00:07:48,600 --> 00:07:53,638
Church. How then could I unite with this wild idoltor in worshiping his piece of

102
00:07:53,639 --> 00:07:59,397
wood? But what is worship? Thought I. Do you suppose now, Ishmael, that the

103
00:07:59,398 --> 00:08:04,839
magnanimous God of heaven and earth, pagans and all included, can possibly be

104
00:08:04,840 --> 00:08:10,839
jealous of an insignificant bit of black wood? Impossible. But what is worship?

105
00:08:10,840 --> 00:08:13,718
To do the will of God. That is worship.

106
00:08:13,719 --> 00:08:16,519
And what is the will of God? To do to my

107
00:08:16,520 --> 00:08:21,397
fellow man what I would have my fellow man do to me. That is the will of God.

108
00:08:21,398 --> 00:08:26,679
Now Quiquake is my fellow man. And what do I wish that this qui quue would do to

109
00:08:26,680 --> 00:08:31,718
me? Why unite with me in my particular Presbyterian form of worship?

110
00:08:31,719 --> 00:08:37,718
Consequently, I must then unite with him in his ergo I must turn idoltor. So I

111
00:08:37,719 --> 00:08:42,358
kindled the shavings, helped prop up the innocent little idol, offered him burnt

112
00:08:42,359 --> 00:08:47,397
biscuit with queue, salammed before him twice or thrice, kissed his nose, and

113
00:08:47,398 --> 00:08:52,039
that done. We undressed and went to bed at peace with our own consciences and

114
00:08:52,040 --> 00:08:57,078
all the world. But we did not go to sleep without some little chat. How it

115
00:08:57,079 --> 00:09:01,317
is, I know not, but there is no place like a bed for confidential disclosures

116
00:09:01,318 --> 00:09:05,958
between friends. Men and wife, they say, there open the very bottom of their

117
00:09:05,959 --> 00:09:10,358
souls to each other, and some old couples often lie and chat over old

118
00:09:10,359 --> 00:09:16,679
times till nearly morning. Thus then, in our hearts honeymoon, lay I in Que, a

119
00:09:16,680 --> 00:09:20,959
cozy, loving pair.
